{"name":"Chilli Crab","hex":"#e93a0e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chilli crab","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dish originated and popularized in Singapore, made up of stir-fried crab cooked in a semi-thick sweet and savoury tomato- and chilli-based sauce."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chilli_crab"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chilli crab","blocks":[{"text":"Chilli crab is a Southeast Asian seafood dish popular in Malaysian and Singaporean cuisines. The modern version of the dish originated in the region in the 1950s. It is typically prepared by stir-frying mud crabs in sweet and savoury, semi-thick sauce made primarily from tomatoes and chilli peppers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Chilli_crab-02.jpg/330px-Chilli_crab-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilli_crab"},{"title":"Crab","blocks":[{"text":"Crabs are decapod crustaceans, either the Brachyura or various groups within the closely related Anomura, characterised by having a heavily armoured shell, their tail segments concealed under the body, the ability to run sideways, and the habit of hiding in rocky crevices. They do not form a single natural group or clade, but have convergently evolved multiple times from the ancestral decapod body plan through carcinisation, the process of creating this set of characteristics. As a group, they are thus polyphyletic, meaning they have multiple evolutionary origins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Cancer_pagurus.jpg/330px-Cancer_pagurus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Horseshoe_crab_%28white_background%29.jpg/500px-Horseshoe_crab_%28white_background%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars